<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grey College, Bloemfontein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1855">1855</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Grey College is a boys' school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, founded in 1855 and named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-grey">Sir George Grey</a>, governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>, who supported its establishment. One of the oldest schools in the former Orange Free State, it gave rise in 1904 to Grey University College, the forerunner of the University of the Free State.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/grey-college-bloemfontein.jpg" alt="Grey College, Bloemfontein" width="152" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boershistory · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grey_college.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The school owes its name and its founding to a substantial donation from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-grey">Sir George Grey</a>, then governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>. It was opened officially on 13 October 1855, and its first headmaster was the Reverend Andrew Murray. Formal education came later to the northern regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> than to the Cape, and Grey College is the oldest school north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange River</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In his inaugural speech as president of the Orange Free State in 1896, Marthinus Theunis Steyn addressed pupils of Grey College and spoke of his hope for a university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a> to which young people from the whole country could come. He supported the idea that the college should offer higher education to the people of the Free State. In 1904 a tertiary section of Grey College was set apart for that purpose, and from it grew the University of the Free State.</p><p>Its former pupils include Steyn himself, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> soldier and politician Deneys Reitz, the writer and adviser Sir Laurens van der Post, the anti-apartheid lawyer Bram Fischer, who defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rivonia-trial">Rivonia Trial</a>, Pierre van Ryneveld, founding commander of the South African Air Force, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a> novelist Etienne Leroux, a leading member of the Sestigers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Grey College is a public boys' school teaching in English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a>, in the suburb of Universitas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a>, and one of the 23 Milner Schools. Five of its historic buildings are provincial heritage sites of the Free State: the Main Building and Hamilton Hall, Andrew Murray House, Brill House and the tuck shop. The school is known above all for rugby and has produced many Springbok players, as well as cricketers who played for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, among them Kepler Wessels and Hansie Cronjé, whose ashes are kept at the school.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Grey College, Bloemfontein” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dutch was the official language and the Dutch Reformed Church the centre of community life, while <b>Grey College</b>, founded in Bloemfontein in 1855, and a growing number of state schools built a modest educational system.</blockquote><a href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
